<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Consequence/1f9d7ea9-2166-4a18-bf4e-2014b7baebd3> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#concerns> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/SystemComponent/e71422b6-0c80-4741-aa68-a27243061168> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Consequence/1f9d7ea9-2166-4a18-bf4e-2014b7baebd3> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "James Adam <jcadam@startmail.com>" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Consequence/1f9d7ea9-2166-4a18-bf4e-2014b7baebd3> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "README.md's Accuracy table \"Reasonable\" column is re-measured against a newer OWL-RL/Reasonable toolchain (owlrl 7.1.4, reasonable 0.4.4), giving new percentages relative to OWL-RL ground truth: BFO -34.3%, Pizza -64.0%, CCO -34.8%, Schema.org +28.7%, Brick +104.2% — replacing the prior snapshot of BFO +91.9%, Pizza +29.1%, CCO -11.4%, Schema.org +15.9%, Brick +81.9%. The qualitative finding (Reasonable diverges far more from OWL-RL ground truth than either Growl or Growl --complete) still holds, and for three of five ontologies (BFO, Pizza, Brick) the magnitude of divergence is now larger, not smaller, than previously reported.\n\nWhy + evidence: commit 84bd3aed29939cf25346325d3fb9f6a778fd6d1f (#20 \"Performance improvements\") README.md diff — the Accuracy table's Reasonable column percentages change as above, and a new paragraph is inserted before the Performance table: \"All three reasoners are run in a single pass on the same machine, so the relative comparison holds regardless of hardware; absolute times are not comparable across machines or runs. Measured with owlrl 7.1.4 and reasonable 0.4.4 — reasoner versions can shift inference counts, so the Reasonable column reflects that release specifically.\"" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
