<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/651bee90-60dc-4bc6-aa05-e0b3f353bf59> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#constrains> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/SystemComponent/833a482a-9217-4511-99e7-1166a8c28fbb> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/651bee90-60dc-4bc6-aa05-e0b3f353bf59> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "James Adam <jcadam@startmail.com>" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/651bee90-60dc-4bc6-aa05-e0b3f353bf59> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "Growl's Rust Arena wrapper must remain !Send + !Sync, but now because each Arena's own internal allocation state is unsynchronized across threads — not because the global intern pool is thread-unsafe, since the intern pool is now protected by an internal pthread_mutex_t (compiled in via SLOP_INTERN_THREADSAFE). Multiple threads may now each create and use their own Arena concurrently without any external caller-side Mutex; a single Arena must still never be shared across threads. Evidence: rust/README.md's rewritten \"Thread Safety\" section (\"The C runtime uses a global intern pool ... that is protected by a `pthread_mutex_t` ... This means multiple arenas can safely be used concurrently from different threads.\" / \"The `Arena` type is still `!Send + !Sync` — individual arenas must not be shared across threads. Each thread should create its own arena.\"); rust/tests/integration.rs drops the static `C_LOCK: Mutex<()>` and every per-test `let _lock = C_LOCK.lock()...` line." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/651bee90-60dc-4bc6-aa05-e0b3f353bf59> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "proposed" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
