<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/eaf0f55f-4d1d-4f41-96fb-d9b5a51b1aae> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "James Adam <jcadam@startmail.com>" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/eaf0f55f-4d1d-4f41-96fb-d9b5a51b1aae> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "Growl's transpiled C thread-support structs (thread_ThreadInt.id, thread_ThreadWithChan.id in csrc/src/slop_thread.h) must store the OS thread handle as pthread_t, matching the runtime's slop_thread_int struct — not a fixed-width uint64_t — because pthread_t is a pointer type (struct _opaque_pthread_t *) on macOS, not an integer, so assigning/casting it into a uint64_t field is a hard compiler error under GCC 14.\n\nWhy + evidence: commit cf8aaf7260 (\"fix macOS release build: use pthread_t for thread ID fields\") changes csrc/src/slop_thread.h's `struct thread_ThreadInt { ...; uint64_t id; ... }` and `struct thread_ThreadWithChan { ...; uint64_t id; ... }` to `pthread_t id` in both, per the commit body: \"On macOS, pthread_t is a pointer type (struct _opaque_pthread_t *), not uint64_t, causing a hard error with GCC 14. Changed thread_ThreadInt.id and thread_ThreadWithChan.id to use pthread_t, matching the runtime's slop_thread_int struct.\"" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/eaf0f55f-4d1d-4f41-96fb-d9b5a51b1aae> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/eaf0f55f-4d1d-4f41-96fb-d9b5a51b1aae> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasTimestamp> "2026-02-09T22:31:23Z"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#dateTime> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
